WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.760
Hello everyone and welcome to my talk on fit happens how secure boot wasn't.

00:06.320 --> 00:11.120
My name is Ahmad Fatoum. I am an embedded Linux developer with Pingotronics. We are a German

00:11.120 --> 00:16.880
Linux consulting company and especially in the last few years most of our customer projects

00:16.880 --> 00:25.200
are employing some verified boot scheme and on the x86 systems this is most often UKIs for us

00:25.200 --> 00:31.920
and on the arm systems we are heavy users of fit and fit is what i'm going to talk about today

00:33.040 --> 00:39.760
so i will start with some history about fit how fit came to increase in scope how signing was

00:39.760 --> 00:46.480
bolted onto the format how it was exploited over the years and then talking about my proposal for

00:46.480 --> 00:53.440
avoiding some of these issues and how we can survive using fit going forward so how did we

00:53.440 --> 00:58.400
get fit so it takes us with a grain of salt i was not there for it i was like in middle school i was

00:58.400 --> 01:05.920
like 13 years old but basically you had uimages and uimage is a very simple format you have a 64

01:05.920 --> 01:12.560
byte headers in front of your image in its last iteration you had these multi-file images where

01:12.560 --> 01:17.600
you had at the start of the image a number of sizes and then you had multiple images in the

01:17.600 --> 01:25.200
same binary and it's not difficult to see where this falls short so you have only a crc32 not

01:25.200 --> 01:32.480
enough space for like a longer cryptographic hash you have only a single load address and

01:32.480 --> 01:38.000
you can't have something like a kernel and the device tree but no ram disk because if you have

01:38.000 --> 01:44.160
a ram disk of zero that terminates this list at the right side at the top so you can't have that

01:44.880 --> 01:50.800
and looking at these limitations there was a need for proper multi-file images that means this

01:50.800 --> 01:57.040
metadata that we had for all these images should really be per image so each image whether it's a

01:57.040 --> 02:02.800
kernel a device tree or a ram disk should have its own load address should have its own hash or crc

02:02.800 --> 02:08.800
or whatever and to actually boot this you want to have bootable configurations so this is a

02:08.800 --> 02:11.860
a combination of images, a subset of them,

02:11.860 --> 02:13.220
or maybe all of them.

02:13.220 --> 02:16.360
And these are what you would boot on an actual board.

02:16.360 --> 02:18.180
So board one needs these three images

02:18.180 --> 02:20.440
and board two needs these two images.

02:20.440 --> 02:23.220
And all of that should exist in the same file.

02:23.220 --> 02:26.040
So you can deploy one file that you target

02:26.040 --> 02:27.660
a lot of different hardware with.

02:28.600 --> 02:31.840
And yeah, that's basically how FIT came to be.

02:31.840 --> 02:34.400
Of course, for it all to be in the same file,

02:34.400 --> 02:35.920
you need a file format.

02:35.920 --> 02:39.660
and device tree was what was chosen for this file format.

02:39.660 --> 02:42.940
And it makes sense because, well,

02:42.940 --> 02:46.100
you had this problem of you image being not flexible,

02:46.100 --> 02:48.160
device tree is infinitely flexible.

02:48.160 --> 02:50.620
You can have arbitrary nesting in your nodes.

02:50.620 --> 02:53.520
I think nowadays it's limited to 64 for fit parsing,

02:53.520 --> 02:56.140
but yeah, you can go down as you like,

02:56.140 --> 02:58.860
and you can have any number of key value pairs

02:58.860 --> 03:01.240
with string property names.

03:01.240 --> 03:03.700
So you can add anything at all.

03:03.700 --> 03:05.160
And it had available tooling,

03:05.160 --> 03:07.280
So there is a device tree compiler even back then.

03:07.280 --> 03:09.540
There is libfttt, nowadays you have a lot

03:09.540 --> 03:11.380
of more tooling for it.

03:11.380 --> 03:14.160
And a very strong argument, especially back then,

03:14.160 --> 03:16.300
was that code size, especially in the bootloader,

03:16.300 --> 03:17.640
comes at a premium.

03:17.640 --> 03:20.900
And if the bootloader already has libfttt

03:20.900 --> 03:23.940
for parsing the device tree that it manipulates

03:23.940 --> 03:26.580
before passing to the kernel, so for example,

03:26.580 --> 03:28.440
the boot arguments on an ARM system

03:28.440 --> 03:29.720
are written into the device tree.

03:29.720 --> 03:32.780
So the bootloader has some device tree support.

03:32.780 --> 03:35.100
So why not use the same device tree support

03:35.100 --> 03:37.800
for the image where the kernel itself is located in.

03:37.800 --> 03:41.460
And yeah, so a lot of birds want stone.

03:41.460 --> 03:43.920
So I think that was the thinking.

03:44.760 --> 03:48.520
And yeah, so that's how it looks like.

03:48.520 --> 03:50.700
You have these images on the right.

03:50.700 --> 03:53.800
You have a kernel, a RAM disk, any number of device trees.

03:53.800 --> 03:56.320
And then on the left side, you have these configurations.

03:56.320 --> 03:57.760
You can have any number of images,

03:57.760 --> 03:59.500
any number of configurations.

04:00.500 --> 04:01.860
I'm not sure if you can have one

04:01.860 --> 04:04.000
without any configurations, but yeah.

04:04.000 --> 04:08.460
you can surely have more than one in any of them.

04:08.460 --> 04:12.120
And yeah, what the bootloader is going to do

04:12.120 --> 04:14.360
is it's going to read this device tree

04:14.360 --> 04:17.740
and determine which configuration it should boot.

04:17.740 --> 04:19.540
So there are multiple ways to do that.

04:19.540 --> 04:20.420
What I like to do

04:20.420 --> 04:23.320
is to use the compatible property of configurations.

04:23.320 --> 04:24.820
So each configuration can say,

04:24.820 --> 04:28.860
I am compatible with Aracha Rock 3A for example.

04:28.860 --> 04:31.080
And the bootloader device tree itself

04:31.080 --> 04:32.760
has a compatible for the board.

04:32.760 --> 04:35.160
So the bootloader can match its own compatible

04:35.160 --> 04:36.980
against the compatible of the fit,

04:36.980 --> 04:38.360
and once it has a match, okay,

04:38.360 --> 04:39.920
this is a configuration I want to boot,

04:39.920 --> 04:41.640
and it's going to boot it.

04:41.640 --> 04:44.640
Now, as you can see in this fit here,

04:44.640 --> 04:46.660
there are a lot of configurations.

04:46.660 --> 04:50.960
419, for example, was the index of the ROC3A.

04:50.960 --> 04:53.080
This is generated by the kernel build.

04:53.080 --> 04:56.080
So since Linux 6.10, I think,

04:56.080 --> 04:59.300
the kernel, the cable supports a make image fit target

04:59.300 --> 05:02.380
on ARM64, and that generates you a fit

05:02.380 --> 05:03.980
with all device trees and the kernel

05:03.980 --> 05:05.960
and optionally a RAM disk.

05:05.960 --> 05:08.720
But you can also write this ITS

05:08.720 --> 05:11.300
for image tree source file by hand.

05:11.300 --> 05:13.540
And there is this ink bin directive.

05:13.540 --> 05:15.760
I think it might have been added, especially for FIT,

05:15.760 --> 05:17.800
where you can include arbitrary binaries

05:17.800 --> 05:19.720
directly into the FIT.

05:19.720 --> 05:22.720
And yeah, but there are also various build system wrappers

05:22.720 --> 05:24.880
in tools like PDXtest or Yocto.

05:24.880 --> 05:26.680
So in Yocto there are two of them.

05:26.680 --> 05:29.040
One in OE core, kernel fit image pb class.

05:29.040 --> 05:30.860
One in meta OE fit image pb class.

05:30.860 --> 05:34.480
These generate for user ITS and then make image

05:34.480 --> 05:36.780
takes this and compiles a fit out of it.

05:36.780 --> 05:40.800
Make image can also on its own generate fit

05:40.800 --> 05:43.160
in that it takes some command line arguments

05:43.160 --> 05:45.580
and then it produces the binary.

05:45.580 --> 05:47.160
This is how the binary looks like.

05:47.160 --> 05:49.360
If you have seen some diagrams of device tree,

05:49.360 --> 05:51.340
it's exactly a device tree.

05:51.340 --> 05:53.060
So there is this memory reservation block

05:53.060 --> 05:55.300
but it doesn't make sense for fit so it's empty.

05:55.300 --> 05:57.720
There is a structure block which contains all nodes

05:57.720 --> 06:00.780
and properties including the values of all your kernels,

06:00.780 --> 06:01.980
device trees and ram disks,

06:01.980 --> 06:03.800
this is all inline as a properties.

06:03.800 --> 06:07.320
This is what I have marked here in green.

06:07.320 --> 06:10.340
And because a number of strings in the properties

06:10.340 --> 06:12.220
appear a lot of times, so for example,

06:12.220 --> 06:14.600
this compatible string in a normal device tree,

06:14.600 --> 06:17.280
it can occur a hundred times or so.

06:17.280 --> 06:18.820
So these strings are in turns,

06:18.820 --> 06:21.220
they are in a separate strings block.

06:21.220 --> 06:22.200
But that's basically it.

06:22.200 --> 06:24.580
You have a header, it has a number of offsets,

06:24.580 --> 06:27.280
and yeah, that's how fit looks like.

06:27.280 --> 06:29.680
And you can use the exact same toolings

06:29.680 --> 06:32.120
that you use for FTT, for flattened device tree,

06:32.120 --> 06:33.360
also for fit.

06:33.360 --> 06:36.380
But the downside here is that you can't detect

06:36.380 --> 06:38.220
if you have an FTT or a fit.

06:38.220 --> 06:39.620
They look exactly the same.

06:39.620 --> 06:40.760
You actually have to pause it

06:40.760 --> 06:42.520
and implement some heuristic,

06:42.520 --> 06:44.300
like checking if,

06:44.300 --> 06:47.060
is there a slash configurations node, for example.

06:47.060 --> 06:48.360
And yeah, you wouldn't see that

06:48.360 --> 06:50.400
in actual hardware device tree,

06:50.400 --> 06:51.760
so that's probably a fit.

06:53.180 --> 06:55.500
Now, fit didn't stop there.

06:55.500 --> 06:59.480
Fit has evolved and increased in scope.

06:59.480 --> 07:01.980
So you can use fit as a partition table.

07:01.980 --> 07:05.720
So why should you put your kernel inside

07:05.720 --> 07:06.700
a device tree property?

07:06.700 --> 07:08.500
Why can't we put it outside

07:08.500 --> 07:11.660
and just have a data offset and a data size?

07:11.660 --> 07:13.540
And yeah, basically a partition table

07:13.540 --> 07:15.240
that you put on the start of your partition

07:15.240 --> 07:18.620
and then you can have the actual image data outside.

07:19.860 --> 07:22.080
It's a bit funny kind of partition table

07:22.080 --> 07:23.980
because you can have interned partitions,

07:23.980 --> 07:26.440
which are just images are inline in the fit

07:26.440 --> 07:29.300
and some external partitions.

07:29.300 --> 07:33.080
But yeah, that is what fit can provide you.

07:33.080 --> 07:36.680
And this also breaks the total FDT size member.

07:36.680 --> 07:39.140
So previously you could read that

07:39.140 --> 07:40.520
and know how big a fit is.

07:40.520 --> 07:41.420
Now you can't anymore.

07:41.420 --> 07:43.840
Now you only know how big the device tree portion

07:43.840 --> 07:44.780
of the fit is,

07:44.780 --> 07:46.360
but you don't know how big the images is.

07:46.360 --> 07:48.960
You actually need to walk through the fit

07:48.960 --> 07:52.160
and pass every image and look at each data offset

07:52.160 --> 07:54.500
and at each data size, add them up

07:54.500 --> 07:57.800
and determine which is the largest extent.

07:57.800 --> 07:59.860
and then you know how big your fit is.

07:59.860 --> 08:01.860
So you can't say if you have a fit,

08:01.860 --> 08:05.360
you can't say how big your fit is

08:05.360 --> 08:08.400
without actually parsing the device tree.

08:08.400 --> 08:13.340
Now, fit didn't stop at being something booted by U-boot

08:13.340 --> 08:15.680
or Bearbox or various other bootloaders.

08:15.680 --> 08:18.880
U-boot itself is nowadays often in a fit.

08:18.880 --> 08:21.260
So you have U-boot SPL,

08:21.260 --> 08:23.320
which needs to load not only U-boot proper

08:23.320 --> 08:24.460
and provided a device tree,

08:24.460 --> 08:27.300
but also maybe trusted firmware A, Opti and so on.

08:27.300 --> 08:29.760
and you need some container format for all of these.

08:29.760 --> 08:33.020
So well, why not use fit, you know, birds and stones.

08:33.020 --> 08:35.400
So there is a small problem here

08:35.400 --> 08:38.800
that SPL is running at the highest privilege level.

08:38.800 --> 08:42.380
So you could maybe mount an attack against you with proper

08:42.380 --> 08:45.980
and it would not be so bad if Opti has already been loaded

08:45.980 --> 08:48.420
so you can't access stuff in your trust zone.

08:48.420 --> 08:50.320
But the SPL is usually running

08:50.320 --> 08:52.740
at the highest available privilege level.

08:52.740 --> 08:57.020
And an unfun fact about this is SPL is very size constrained.

08:57.020 --> 09:00.700
So some checks in libfdt that check

09:00.700 --> 09:04.820
if a device tree is malformed are skipped in the SPL

09:04.820 --> 09:08.940
for size reasons, although it is the more critical part.

09:08.940 --> 09:10.520
But they are there in uBoot proper,

09:10.520 --> 09:13.700
so you are safe there if this is some issue

09:13.700 --> 09:15.100
that you could exploit.

09:17.200 --> 09:20.780
Yeah, so how did fit gain signatures?

09:20.780 --> 09:25.400
So at the very first, fit only had like hashes and CRCs

09:25.400 --> 09:27.160
you could just write into the device tree,

09:27.160 --> 09:29.840
my algorithm is CRC32, this is the value.

09:29.840 --> 09:31.160
You had no kind of signatures,

09:31.160 --> 09:34.220
this came after maybe five years after it.

09:34.220 --> 09:38.740
And at first, it was signing for images.

09:38.740 --> 09:40.360
That means you have this image node,

09:40.360 --> 09:41.840
it has this data property,

09:41.840 --> 09:43.580
and this data property contains for your kernel,

09:43.580 --> 09:46.760
for example, and what's being signed is exactly that.

09:46.760 --> 09:49.400
This data property, the content of this data property,

09:49.400 --> 09:51.500
nothing else in the node is being signed,

09:51.500 --> 09:53.580
including the load and entry address.

09:53.580 --> 09:55.640
And this is very critical

09:55.640 --> 09:59.340
because you can just put your unsigned payload anywhere,

09:59.340 --> 10:01.280
for example, in a new property,

10:01.280 --> 10:03.340
just after the data property,

10:03.340 --> 10:05.460
increment your entry to jump to it

10:05.460 --> 10:06.840
and nothing will stop you.

10:06.840 --> 10:08.680
U-Boot has no bounce checking on entry.

10:08.680 --> 10:11.840
So you can jump anywhere, basically not, yeah.

10:11.840 --> 10:14.420
So anywhere within the fit that's loaded, you can jump.

10:14.420 --> 10:16.920
Probably you can also jump outside the fit.

10:16.920 --> 10:19.700
And you could add bounce checking on entry,

10:19.700 --> 10:22.420
but this wouldn't help because this is a kernel image.

10:22.420 --> 10:26.380
it's like 10 megabytes of executable code or 20 megabytes,

10:26.380 --> 10:28.440
you will have so many jump instructions there

10:28.440 --> 10:30.480
that you could jump to and then continue

10:30.480 --> 10:32.060
to jump to your payload.

10:32.060 --> 10:35.580
So this is fundamentally insecure and you would never,

10:35.580 --> 10:38.360
I don't think you can salvage it.

10:38.360 --> 10:40.900
I wasn't aware of this at first until I read about it

10:40.900 --> 10:41.880
on the uBoot mailing list.

10:41.880 --> 10:44.920
So Barebox never implemented it because as I understand,

10:44.920 --> 10:47.020
there were fears of mix and match attacks

10:47.020 --> 10:48.840
that you take a device tree and the kernel

10:48.840 --> 10:50.260
and mix and match them.

10:50.260 --> 10:53.420
But this is really a very minor issue

10:54.900 --> 10:58.480
next to what you can do with the entry here.

10:58.480 --> 11:01.240
And yeah, so who does it?

11:01.240 --> 11:03.620
UiCoreUbootSignBB class does it if you ask it

11:03.620 --> 11:05.520
with SPL sign enable.

11:05.520 --> 11:07.820
That means TFR, OPT, FDT, and Uboot,

11:07.820 --> 11:09.640
if you are using that, yeah,

11:09.640 --> 11:12.480
they can be trivially exchanged

11:12.480 --> 11:15.300
and you should probably implement

11:15.300 --> 11:20.220
some downgrade protection, fixes, revoke your signing keys.

11:20.220 --> 11:22.660
So yeah, that's bad.

11:22.660 --> 11:25.180
I reported it and Marta is currently fixing it.

11:25.180 --> 11:27.800
I think the series is now at version three or version four.

11:27.800 --> 11:29.300
Here is a link to that.

11:29.300 --> 11:31.680
Make image itself happily generates it for you

11:31.680 --> 11:33.360
when you use minus F auto.

11:33.360 --> 11:36.480
So people use minus F auto because a side effect

11:36.480 --> 11:39.580
of using it is that it can patch the UBoot device tree.

11:39.580 --> 11:42.220
Okay, you can do that, but do not use the fit

11:42.220 --> 11:45.360
that it generates because it's not secure.

11:45.360 --> 11:48.460
And there are a number of upstream UBoot SPLs

11:48.460 --> 11:51.140
that do board fit image post process,

11:51.140 --> 11:54.760
which allow, and SOC code,

11:54.760 --> 11:57.380
this is a weak function, so SOC code can implement it.

11:57.380 --> 12:01.240
This allows SOC code to implement some custom way

12:01.240 --> 12:03.220
to verify the fit.

12:03.220 --> 12:04.980
I believe that's wrong.

12:04.980 --> 12:08.060
I didn't look into it, but probably someone should.

12:08.060 --> 12:11.400
So on ARM64, actually, the entry is ignored,

12:11.400 --> 12:14.020
but you can change OS to phawxworks,

12:14.020 --> 12:15.380
which is enabled by default,

12:15.380 --> 12:18.400
and then you can use entry again.

12:18.400 --> 12:21.640
So yeah, it's not good.

12:21.640 --> 12:23.520
What you all are hopefully using,

12:23.520 --> 12:26.640
or maybe something better, is sign-in configurations.

12:26.640 --> 12:30.040
And in this case, the hash also only covers the data,

12:30.040 --> 12:33.380
but critically, the configuration has the signature

12:33.380 --> 12:37.020
and it covers not only, yeah, it covers a number of nodes.

12:37.020 --> 12:40.000
So it covers the root node, you see this in this hash node,

12:40.000 --> 12:43.000
it covers slash, it covers slash configurations on fun,

12:43.000 --> 12:45.880
it covers your images, all images you have,

12:45.880 --> 12:49.580
and that hash image, and you should be protected this way.

12:49.580 --> 12:51.740
I talked about this string interning

12:51.740 --> 12:53.620
that happens for property names.

12:53.620 --> 12:56.460
So yeah, you need to hash the strings also,

12:56.460 --> 12:59.280
but only a prefix of strings is hashed

12:59.280 --> 13:01.620
because you can add configurations of that effect.

13:01.620 --> 13:02.740
They can have property names

13:02.740 --> 13:04.120
that are not in the string table.

13:04.120 --> 13:06.340
So you hash from the start of the string table

13:06.340 --> 13:09.400
until some point and don't hash the rest.

13:10.840 --> 13:12.980
But that's how the format is.

13:12.980 --> 13:14.940
And yeah, you can't change entry anymore

13:14.940 --> 13:16.940
with configuration signing,

13:16.940 --> 13:18.340
but you can add extra images,

13:18.340 --> 13:19.880
you can add extra configurations,

13:19.880 --> 13:22.000
you can add a property to slash images,

13:22.000 --> 13:23.740
but not to the image node itself.

13:23.740 --> 13:25.740
So there are still some degrees of freedom

13:25.740 --> 13:26.920
that you can use.

13:26.920 --> 13:28.140
This is how it looks like.

13:28.140 --> 13:30.500
So the FTT headers that we passed at start

13:30.500 --> 13:32.820
is completely unchecked.

13:32.820 --> 13:34.380
The stuff that's dashed,

13:34.380 --> 13:37.460
there is only enforced that this node exists,

13:37.460 --> 13:39.840
but not what it has as contents.

13:39.840 --> 13:43.280
And the red stuff, yeah, the red stuff is verified,

13:43.280 --> 13:45.900
that the gray stuff is completely unverified.

13:45.900 --> 13:47.500
So normally, if everything is correct,

13:47.500 --> 13:52.180
it shouldn't matter, but things rarely are.

13:52.180 --> 13:56.540
So both barebox and uboot are written in C,

13:56.540 --> 14:00.260
and they have the same issues many C code bases have.

14:00.260 --> 14:02.360
Yeah, a lot of undefined behavior.

14:02.360 --> 14:03.760
These are only the fixes.

14:04.680 --> 14:06.580
I will leave it as an exercise to find out

14:06.580 --> 14:08.920
how much more of these might be there.

14:10.220 --> 14:11.260
Yeah, fit happens.

14:11.260 --> 14:13.700
So let's talk about some fit exploits.

14:13.700 --> 14:15.300
So fit is tricky to pass.

14:15.300 --> 14:18.760
And this can be a bit unexpected

14:18.760 --> 14:21.300
because hey, it's just a device tree.

14:21.300 --> 14:23.320
But you do pass this device tree

14:23.320 --> 14:24.860
and you pass a lot of it

14:24.860 --> 14:27.220
before you actually verify the signatures.

14:27.220 --> 14:29.280
And an attacker has just one goal,

14:29.280 --> 14:31.460
get fit consumers to confuse nodes.

14:31.460 --> 14:34.000
So you can add your own nodes, we have seen that.

14:34.000 --> 14:38.060
You just need to have one place just confuse these nodes.

14:38.060 --> 14:41.420
So for example, if you have kernel1 in lowercase

14:41.420 --> 14:45.100
and kernel1 in uppercase that you insert after the fact

14:45.100 --> 14:47.320
and you have a signed configuration,

14:47.320 --> 14:50.560
you will have these two parts of the code.

14:50.560 --> 14:54.040
What was signed, so I can verify it,

14:54.040 --> 14:55.780
and what should I boot?

14:55.780 --> 15:00.240
So if you can abuse difference in APIs used for one thing

15:00.240 --> 15:02.240
and for the other thing,

15:02.240 --> 15:05.720
then maybe you got yourself a boot bypass.

15:06.560 --> 15:09.820
And yeah, there are numerous ways you could do that.

15:09.820 --> 15:12.280
We have seen memory safety issues.

15:12.280 --> 15:15.900
Perhaps you can get some buffer overflow or something.

15:15.900 --> 15:18.920
Fuzziness in the API themselves.

15:18.920 --> 15:22.020
So Uboot is using libftt,

15:22.020 --> 15:24.800
barebox is using these of underscore functions

15:24.800 --> 15:26.980
that originally were ported from the kernel,

15:26.980 --> 15:29.180
but both of them were not originally written

15:29.180 --> 15:31.080
to handle untrusted input.

15:31.080 --> 15:35.800
I believe fit might be the only untrusted device tree

15:35.800 --> 15:37.740
that's actually being used.

15:37.740 --> 15:40.820
So TFA, if it takes a device tree, it trusts it

15:40.820 --> 15:44.420
because the first stage bootloader running, the BL2,

15:44.420 --> 15:46.260
it's already running at the highest privilege level.

15:46.260 --> 15:48.540
It doesn't need to protect itself against it.

15:48.540 --> 15:50.180
It's the same thing as a kernel is running

15:50.180 --> 15:52.500
at the same privilege level as a bootloader booting it.

15:52.500 --> 15:54.160
It doesn't make sense to assume

15:54.160 --> 15:56.720
that the bootloader is malicious, you know?

15:56.720 --> 15:59.080
And yeah, it's the job of the bootloader

15:59.080 --> 16:05.720
pass something correct here and so you have apis in libftt for example where you say give me a

16:05.720 --> 16:11.240
memory node and it gives you a memory node or a memory at zero node to make it easier for you and

16:11.880 --> 16:17.160
there are in the of family of functions from the kernel functions where you tell it give me this

16:17.160 --> 16:23.560
node with name kernel and it gives you one an uppercase if it doesn't found the lowercase one

16:23.560 --> 16:29.480
and this sounds like trivial stuff but both of these were full fit signature bypass exploits

16:29.480 --> 16:37.320
so unit addresses broke uboot fit verification completely but not barebox case sensitivity

16:37.320 --> 16:43.560
broke barebox but not uboot so there is something here for everyone and yeah you could try to trick

16:43.560 --> 16:49.400
walkers for example you could add slash in node names this shouldn't happen there but are all

16:49.400 --> 16:53.560
all your APIs expecting that they can handle a slash.

16:54.480 --> 16:57.260
You could have non-null terminated strings

16:57.260 --> 16:59.600
and maybe one API doesn't like this at all

16:59.600 --> 17:02.420
and the other API, okay, yeah, no problem.

17:02.420 --> 17:05.980
I will just copy the bytes because there is a size field.

17:05.980 --> 17:09.340
And there is this, let's call it necessary untrusted data

17:09.340 --> 17:11.800
which you are just accessing and assuming, yeah,

17:11.800 --> 17:13.480
an attacker can't do anything with it.

17:13.480 --> 17:16.100
So the default configuration booted by a fit,

17:16.100 --> 17:17.240
the attacker can choose that.

17:17.240 --> 17:19.220
Of course, it must be a verified configuration

17:19.220 --> 17:20.940
but the attacker can choose that.

17:20.940 --> 17:25.220
And hashed nodes, which we have seen, is also untrusted.

17:25.220 --> 17:26.780
Which brings us to this issue,

17:26.780 --> 17:30.740
which is probably the biggest fit security vulnerability

17:30.740 --> 17:35.500
for the last few years, CVE 2026 and so on.

17:35.500 --> 17:38.720
And it's deceptively simple.

17:38.720 --> 17:41.540
So you had this verified kernel image.

17:41.540 --> 17:44.080
It's completely okay, it's good.

17:44.080 --> 17:46.020
And you rename it, you move it

17:46.020 --> 17:49.080
to slash images slash images kernel one.

17:49.080 --> 17:51.580
And then you add a new slash images kernel one

17:51.580 --> 17:54.020
which is your evil payload and then hash node

17:54.020 --> 17:55.460
because it's not being hashed.

17:55.460 --> 17:57.380
It's just tells you what to hash

17:57.380 --> 18:00.840
but itself is not hashed or covered by any signature.

18:00.840 --> 18:03.680
You change slash images kernel one which is,

18:05.720 --> 18:06.760
yeah, this one.

18:06.760 --> 18:09.540
This one used to say slash images kernel one

18:09.540 --> 18:13.360
but now you have changed it and so you hash the old one

18:13.360 --> 18:14.860
but actually boots a new one.

18:14.860 --> 18:19.220
And this way you got yourself full FITS signature bypass

18:19.220 --> 18:22.080
without abusing any memory safety issues.

18:22.080 --> 18:26.500
So this is something that's very general in applicability.

18:26.500 --> 18:28.700
This has fixed now, so the problem here was

18:28.700 --> 18:30.320
that there were two different ways,

18:30.320 --> 18:32.680
one to how to determine what to hash

18:32.680 --> 18:34.840
and how to determine what to boot.

18:34.840 --> 18:38.620
And actually there were the redundancy here in the node

18:38.620 --> 18:42.100
because the configuration node already has a kernel,

18:42.100 --> 18:46.300
FDT and so on properties that point at the images.

18:46.300 --> 18:48.760
And yeah, you should actually check these

18:48.760 --> 18:50.920
because these are used for booting.

18:50.920 --> 18:53.340
Hash nodes is not something you should check

18:53.340 --> 18:54.420
in the bootloader.

18:54.420 --> 18:56.560
This was fixed in uboot, in barebox,

18:56.560 --> 18:58.220
in the fit specification.

18:58.220 --> 19:01.500
And yeah, I think that's everyone who is affected

19:01.500 --> 19:03.900
because I think no one implements configuration signing

19:03.900 --> 19:06.860
outside of uboot and barebox, I'm not sure.

19:06.860 --> 19:08.860
Yeah, so there is an advisory that I have written

19:08.860 --> 19:12.120
that goes a bit in depth what is affected.

19:12.120 --> 19:15.460
So if you're using configuration signing in the U-Boot SPL,

19:15.460 --> 19:19.800
your opt is also probably, how do you call it,

19:19.800 --> 19:21.600
attacked, attackable by this.

19:23.620 --> 19:27.920
Yeah, and I am interested how good publicity work

19:27.920 --> 19:28.780
was done for this.

19:28.780 --> 19:31.040
So please raise your hand if you have a verified boot

19:31.040 --> 19:32.180
project using fit.

19:34.200 --> 19:36.840
And keep your hand up if you have not heard

19:36.840 --> 19:38.720
of this particular issue.

19:39.660 --> 19:41.820
Okay, yeah.

19:41.820 --> 19:45.540
I wasn't able to see how many hands went down.

19:45.540 --> 19:48.260
But yes, a number of people haven't heard about this issue,

19:48.260 --> 19:50.640
so we should have done better work here,

19:50.640 --> 19:52.340
which I will get to.

19:52.340 --> 19:55.540
So, how do we trim the fit?

19:55.540 --> 19:57.160
Yeah, so fit keeps on growing.

19:57.160 --> 19:59.920
So I showed this partition table thing.

19:59.920 --> 20:02.640
You can have partitions that alias each other.

20:02.640 --> 20:04.760
You can have fit as a partition table with the mverity.

20:04.760 --> 20:07.320
So you actually have a kernel driver

20:07.320 --> 20:10.760
that mounts your fit as a partition table

20:10.760 --> 20:12.320
and has variety on it.

20:12.320 --> 20:14.080
So yeah, you can do that.

20:14.080 --> 20:15.600
This is in the spec.

20:15.600 --> 20:17.180
U-boot support is being added.

20:18.480 --> 20:21.980
So our open pull requests for all sorts of things.

20:21.980 --> 20:26.460
And yeah, we should try to keep fit secure,

20:26.460 --> 20:29.220
but not at the cost of new features, right?

20:29.220 --> 20:31.460
This is a slide that I had last year

20:31.460 --> 20:33.840
in my bootloaders under fire talk.

20:33.840 --> 20:37.240
And this was like a wishlist for barebox or in general.

20:37.240 --> 20:39.480
And I'm happy that a lot of this materialized.

20:39.480 --> 20:41.640
So security policies are upstream.

20:41.640 --> 20:45.460
OSS fast has barebox and uboot support now.

20:45.460 --> 20:47.300
This is nice.

20:47.300 --> 20:49.620
The fit code is also being fast.

20:49.620 --> 20:52.720
I think for uboot it fails whenever you need to hash.

20:52.720 --> 20:55.360
So an attacker can trivially compute a hash.

20:55.360 --> 20:59.560
So it's not cool if the father can't continue there.

20:59.560 --> 21:02.980
For barebox or for both of them, barebox and uboot,

21:02.980 --> 21:05.440
You can't actually find this issue that I have shown

21:05.440 --> 21:07.040
because it's not a memory safety issue.

21:07.040 --> 21:08.620
So there is still some work to do here.

21:08.620 --> 21:10.380
I have been trying to do it for barebox.

21:10.380 --> 21:11.420
Have something promising.

21:11.420 --> 21:14.820
I hope that I can upstream it in the future

21:14.820 --> 21:18.900
so we can actually catch these confusion attacks by fuzzing.

21:19.840 --> 21:20.680
There's a barebox thread model.

21:20.680 --> 21:22.900
And yeah, there was this one here.

21:22.900 --> 21:25.440
Fit images, verify full image before parsing.

21:25.440 --> 21:27.620
When I wrote this, I was thinking about memory safety issue.

21:27.620 --> 21:31.060
I was not thinking about like the barn door

21:31.060 --> 21:32.360
being so wide open.

21:33.260 --> 21:35.080
But yeah, so let's get to that.

21:35.080 --> 21:37.860
A lot of stuff has a check mark on it.

21:37.860 --> 21:39.460
What do we need for whole fit signing?

21:39.460 --> 21:41.960
So this is not a new radical new idea.

21:41.960 --> 21:44.860
You have this on uBoot already for the IMX8M.

21:44.860 --> 21:47.560
You sign the whole fit image as if it were firmware

21:47.560 --> 21:49.080
and then you give it the boot ROM.

21:49.080 --> 21:52.100
It's a bit special and it abuses the fact that you can,

21:52.100 --> 21:54.060
after the fit header, just move the nodes

21:54.060 --> 21:56.580
because there are pointers and then you put something there

21:56.580 --> 21:57.680
that lets the boot ROM sync.

21:57.680 --> 22:00.040
Oh, this is a bootloader, I can verify that.

22:00.040 --> 22:02.100
Yeah, but you can do it, people do it,

22:02.100 --> 22:06.880
and the people who did it were not affected by this issue.

22:06.880 --> 22:08.860
So, but they were probably affected

22:08.860 --> 22:09.920
when booting the kernel

22:09.920 --> 22:12.380
because this is often used only in UBoot SPL.

22:12.380 --> 22:16.600
So kernel is, EL2 is compromised, EL3 isn't.

22:16.600 --> 22:18.640
But well, let's get back.

22:18.640 --> 22:19.940
Android verified boot.

22:19.940 --> 22:22.680
You can have this trailer to verify the fit image

22:22.680 --> 22:25.020
as a whole, so it's not a new thing.

22:25.020 --> 22:26.920
The UBoot implementation, when I looked into it,

22:26.920 --> 22:29.160
is time of check, time of use vulnerable.

22:29.160 --> 22:31.900
So don't get running and say,

22:31.900 --> 22:33.600
yeah, I will use full fit signing

22:33.600 --> 22:34.660
and I will take the first thing

22:34.660 --> 22:36.040
that's implemented in uBoot.

22:36.040 --> 22:38.140
That's not a good idea yet.

22:38.140 --> 22:39.500
One thing that's implemented in uBoot

22:39.500 --> 22:42.040
that you could actually use is config image preloadsig.

22:42.040 --> 22:43.980
This has been there for four years now.

22:43.980 --> 22:45.540
It's unfortunately not in the spec.

22:45.540 --> 22:47.140
It's barely documented.

22:47.140 --> 22:49.680
And I think it's not backwards compatible.

22:49.680 --> 22:51.640
So that wouldn't help adoption.

22:51.640 --> 22:53.020
So what do I think is needed?

22:53.020 --> 22:55.220
We need something generic, must be documented,

22:55.220 --> 22:56.320
must be backwards compatible,

22:56.320 --> 22:58.920
so people can actually use it.

22:58.920 --> 23:02.100
And yeah, there's a lot of things you can do with fit,

23:02.100 --> 23:04.260
but we should really focus on the main use case

23:04.260 --> 23:07.880
for most of us, which is a secure kernel boot container.

23:07.880 --> 23:09.980
And I want this format to be attractive.

23:09.980 --> 23:12.680
So people use it even if they are not really sold

23:12.680 --> 23:14.300
on the whole fit signing idea.

23:14.300 --> 23:16.960
So the format should allow you to detect a fit.

23:16.960 --> 23:18.820
It should allow you to know the size of the fit.

23:18.820 --> 23:20.460
It should allow you to append metadata

23:20.460 --> 23:22.740
that's not being verified.

23:22.740 --> 23:27.100
And very critically, it should not do any device reparsing

23:27.100 --> 23:29.220
before verification, because then I think

23:29.220 --> 23:30.580
you can just throw it away.

23:32.160 --> 23:33.880
Yeah, so this is what I came up with.

23:33.880 --> 23:35.720
You have the struct FTT header.

23:35.720 --> 23:38.780
This has this boot CPU ID field,

23:38.780 --> 23:40.480
which is not used nowadays.

23:40.480 --> 23:43.940
And I propose that we add fit as a magic there.

23:43.940 --> 23:47.280
And when there is fit there, we would expect,

23:47.280 --> 23:48.560
or the bootloader would expect

23:48.560 --> 23:50.860
there is a struct fit header directly after it.

23:50.860 --> 23:53.200
It would have also a fit for cross-checking.

23:53.200 --> 23:56.080
And then it would have actually external data size.

23:56.080 --> 23:57.480
so we can actually size a fit

23:57.480 --> 23:59.820
and we need that to be able to hash the fit.

23:59.820 --> 24:02.080
So without knowing how big a fit is,

24:02.080 --> 24:04.420
how should a bootloader know what to hash?

24:04.420 --> 24:05.820
And then it can have some flags

24:05.820 --> 24:08.060
for backwards and forward compatibility.

24:08.060 --> 24:10.180
And if you have a fit header,

24:10.180 --> 24:12.200
you also have a metadata trailer,

24:12.200 --> 24:16.440
which is just a very simple TLV format at the end

24:16.440 --> 24:18.600
where you can add detached signatures

24:18.600 --> 24:21.260
or something else that shouldn't be covered by a hash,

24:21.260 --> 24:23.240
but it should be very simple.

24:23.240 --> 24:27.220
So in comparison, you just read, I think,

24:27.220 --> 24:29.480
like eight fixed offset integers.

24:29.480 --> 24:32.160
The 64-bit ones of them are kept at 48 bits.

24:32.160 --> 24:34.740
So even if you do not do overflow checking,

24:34.740 --> 24:37.040
just check the upper two bytes.

24:37.040 --> 24:39.180
And yeah, hash it, verify it,

24:39.180 --> 24:41.140
and only then do the parsing.

24:41.140 --> 24:45.460
And yeah, in the old style with configurations,

24:45.460 --> 24:46.640
what you see on the left,

24:46.640 --> 24:51.020
the verifying the signature is just step four

24:51.020 --> 24:53.280
instead of being like something you do

24:53.280 --> 24:55.880
before the device tree is being passed.

24:55.880 --> 24:59.220
And I think this has a pretty good compatibility story.

24:59.220 --> 25:01.420
So if you have a new bootloader and an old fit,

25:01.420 --> 25:03.940
no problem, pair configuration signatures

25:03.940 --> 25:06.640
can be supported for a transitory period.

25:06.640 --> 25:08.700
If you have an old bootloader and a new fit,

25:08.700 --> 25:11.020
no problem, because it looks exactly the same.

25:11.020 --> 25:13.860
The FTT header is something added after the,

25:13.860 --> 25:16.320
the fit header is something added after the FTT header,

25:16.320 --> 25:17.560
which you are allowed to do.

25:17.560 --> 25:19.020
If you have a new bootloader and a new fit,

25:19.020 --> 25:20.920
you can use whole fit signatures.

25:20.920 --> 25:24.340
And you would roll this out in the field,

25:24.340 --> 25:27.460
sign both configurations and the whole fit image.

25:27.460 --> 25:28.840
And after a transitory period,

25:28.840 --> 25:31.900
you would stop allowing configuration signing

25:31.900 --> 25:33.940
as a bootloader policy.

25:33.940 --> 25:36.160
And yeah, this is something I would like to see

25:36.160 --> 25:37.560
in my customer projects.

25:38.400 --> 25:41.860
So I wrote a spec draft for that.

25:43.320 --> 25:45.560
Yeah, so there was some discussions with Simon.

25:45.560 --> 25:48.100
I think we reached a consensus in this approach

25:48.100 --> 25:50.400
that we will add this TLV.

25:50.400 --> 25:52.740
This TLV overlaps a bit with device tree

25:52.740 --> 25:54.300
but I think this is unavoidable

25:54.300 --> 25:56.880
because if we do something device-tree compatible

25:56.880 --> 25:58.820
people will just use libfttt.

25:58.820 --> 26:01.700
And yeah, I don't want any intern strings

26:01.700 --> 26:04.320
and this whole complexity of device tree

26:04.320 --> 26:06.220
before we do a verification.

26:07.080 --> 26:08.700
Not that you can't get it secure

26:08.700 --> 26:12.360
but experience has shown that this is rather tricky

26:12.360 --> 26:14.340
and we should draw a line

26:14.340 --> 26:16.660
and try to do better in my opinion.

26:17.720 --> 26:19.320
Yeah, so ball is in my court.

26:19.320 --> 26:21.040
I have been busy this week,

26:21.040 --> 26:26.040
but I hope to soon update the PR for feedback.

26:26.480 --> 26:28.840
And after that, we will discuss signature entry

26:28.840 --> 26:31.040
and implementation on the GitHub pull request.

26:31.040 --> 26:32.640
You can click there.

26:32.640 --> 26:34.340
PR44 is its number.

26:34.340 --> 26:37.420
So if you are interested in collaboration or sponsoring,

26:37.420 --> 26:39.100
please let me know.

26:39.100 --> 26:41.900
So what should we do till then?

26:41.900 --> 26:44.940
And more importantly, what should we do when we have it?

26:44.940 --> 26:46.900
So vulnerabilities will happen.

26:46.900 --> 26:54.260
it's independent of which format we are using i think so bootloader updates need to be easier

26:54.260 --> 27:01.140
so most my most our projects are using rauc rauc has a wider repertoire of bootloader update methods

27:01.140 --> 27:06.180
so if you are using rauc or your bootloader is allowing you to update sorry your update

27:06.180 --> 27:11.860
system is allowing you to update the bootload and a boot power fail safe manner you should really do

27:11.860 --> 27:17.860
so but i think also on the bootloader development side there are some process changes that are

27:17.860 --> 27:24.580
desirable so these issues i spent a lot of time looking at them and i can't expect some user of

27:24.580 --> 27:29.220
the bootloader to get the same understanding what the implications are by reading a commit message

27:29.220 --> 27:33.620
so for these critical issues i wrote security advisories and they were published on the

27:33.620 --> 27:39.220
barebox github yeah one issue was specific to barebox the other also affected uboots so i wrote

27:39.220 --> 27:44.180
a security advisory so it's applicable to both and got a you would from the you would maintainers

27:44.900 --> 27:51.220
a sign off on it we started requesting cves so cve requesting and bootloader land is something that

27:51.220 --> 27:57.140
a security researcher does if they want to but no one does but yeah i think if you are writing

27:57.140 --> 28:01.380
such a nice severe nice advisory as people have told me you want people to read it so you should

28:01.380 --> 28:07.380
request a cve so people have a reason to go there and yeah we have been maintaining an lts release

28:07.380 --> 28:14.020
for barebox so yokto rhinos includes barebox in open embedded core and this release is lts and

28:14.020 --> 28:18.100
we are backporting patches to there whether there are critical security patches or just normal bug

28:18.100 --> 28:23.780
fixes and for the small versions between them we are having migration guides so we have been doing

28:23.780 --> 28:29.060
this for maybe under two years a year and a half so we have monthly barebox releases there is a

28:29.060 --> 28:33.540
migration guide telling you what you might need to look out for and i really would like to see

28:33.540 --> 28:38.200
like for Opti and other projects because, yeah.

28:38.200 --> 28:39.660
So it's just a small point,

28:39.660 --> 28:41.800
but I want to talk about this a bit

28:41.800 --> 28:45.160
because I think it's really good for security to have it

28:45.160 --> 28:48.940
because there are these defaults in various software

28:48.940 --> 28:51.260
which were chosen at one time

28:51.260 --> 28:54.100
and then it has shown that these are bad defaults.

28:54.100 --> 28:55.260
And now you can change them

28:55.260 --> 28:57.600
because you will break all these users

28:57.600 --> 29:01.620
and all these users wouldn't know why and so on.

29:01.620 --> 29:06.260
And this inconveniences possibly more future users

29:06.260 --> 29:10.580
than there are existing users, just to keep this default.

29:10.580 --> 29:12.580
And I think with migration guides,

29:12.580 --> 29:16.380
we got more bold in bare box to just say you,

29:16.380 --> 29:20.180
there was some, for example, there is boot or boot M

29:20.180 --> 29:24.040
allows you to temporarily raise the level

29:24.040 --> 29:25.640
of fit signature checking.

29:25.640 --> 29:28.520
So there is a global toggle, a build time toggle

29:28.520 --> 29:29.900
and a runtime global toggle.

29:29.900 --> 29:32.740
and then you could, at shell level, raise it.

29:32.740 --> 29:35.520
And this is a feature that might be nice for development,

29:35.520 --> 29:38.820
but it complicates so much thinking about

29:38.820 --> 29:41.020
what level am I at now?

29:41.020 --> 29:42.940
And how do these things interact?

29:42.940 --> 29:44.380
And we just ripped it out.

29:44.380 --> 29:46.080
We wrote in the migration notes,

29:46.080 --> 29:48.040
hey, if you are using this, use this.

29:48.040 --> 29:50.140
We found this is a bad idea, don't use it.

29:50.140 --> 29:52.820
And I would really like more projects to be bold.

29:52.820 --> 29:53.880
So we are not bold.

29:53.880 --> 29:55.460
We still have very old hardware

29:55.460 --> 29:58.660
that I am too emotionally invested to throw out.

29:58.660 --> 30:01.160
but at least a bit.

30:01.160 --> 30:02.600
So in the security relevant stuff,

30:02.600 --> 30:06.460
we should really get into the habit of changing defaults.

30:06.460 --> 30:11.460
And yeah, so I have two more slides and it's about testing.

30:11.880 --> 30:16.220
So I think testing for pitfalls with fit is too difficult.

30:16.220 --> 30:19.520
And who of us even tests that an unsigned fit

30:19.520 --> 30:21.260
is indeed refused?

30:21.260 --> 30:23.920
So this might, great.

30:23.920 --> 30:27.700
Okay, sorry, again, how many were that?

30:27.700 --> 30:31.460
Okay, so six people are doing it, this is good.

30:32.340 --> 30:34.180
Yeah, I wish more people would do it

30:34.180 --> 30:35.800
and that it would be easier to do it

30:35.800 --> 30:38.140
